            <ArticleTitle>Assessment of coronary bypass graft patency by first-line multi-detector computed tomography.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>284-92</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ancard.2014.08.011</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0003-3928(14)00118-8</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The purpose of the study was to assess whether a strategy based on a MDCT performed routinely before CA can reduce the radiation dose during the CA, without increased global exposure in patients who need imaging of CABG. A total of 147 consecutive patients were included. The radiation dose during CA (KAP 12.1 vs 22.0 Gy/cm(2), P&lt;.01) and the volume of iodinated contrast (155 vs 200 mL, P&lt;.02) were reduced when preceded by a MDCT. Patients' cumulative exposures were not different in the 2 strategies (5.0 vs 5.1 mSv, P=.76). MDCT performed in first line is a valuable strategy for the assessment of CABG.</AbstractText>
